q(1,'🟡','sum_two',"Sum of two integers without + or -. `a` and `b` are integers with |a|, |b| <= 10^9. Compute a + b using only bitwise operators and shifts, and return `[sum, rounds]`, where `rounds` is how many times this loop body runs: start with `a &= 0xFFFFFFFF; b &= 0xFFFFFFFF` and while `b` is nonzero, replace `(a, b)` by `((a ^ b) & 0xFFFFFFFF, ((a & b) << 1) & 0xFFFFFFFF)`. When the loop ends convert back to a signed value (if `a > 0x7FFFFFFF` the result is `~(a ^ 0xFFFFFFFF)`). Explain what XOR (sum without carry) and AND shifted left (the carries) mean, why the masking is needed in Python (a negative operand has infinitely many leading 1 bits, so without the mask the loop can fail to terminate, for example for a = -1 and b = 1; show that) and why the result fits in 32 bits for this input range (|a + b| <= 2 * 10^9 < 2^31). ",
 "a 32-bit ripple-carry adder built from single bits",[dict(a=7,b=5),dict(a=-3,b=3),dict(a=10**9,b=10**9),dict(a=-10**9,b=-10**9),dict(a=0,b=0)],BMG+" | "+PY)
q(1,'🟡','bitmask_enum',"Enumerate subsets with a bitmask, and submasks. `items` has 0 <= len <= 12 distinct integers (any sign, |value| <= 100) and `target` is an integer. Using the mask `m` in 0..2^n - 1 where bit i means 'take items[i]', return `[found, pairs]`: `found` is the list of all subsets (each a list in the order of `items`) whose sum equals `target`, listed in increasing order of the mask value (so the empty subset, mask 0, comes first when target is 0); `pairs` is the number of (mask, submask) pairs visited when, for every mask m, you loop `sub = m; while sub: ...; sub = (sub - 1) & m` (non-empty submasks only). Explain why `(sub - 1) & m` visits every non-empty submask of `m` exactly once in decreasing order, and why `pairs` equals 3^n - 2^n (each item is in the submask, in the mask but not the submask, or in neither; subtract the empty submask of every mask). Say when a bitmask is better than recursion (small n, set as an integer, fast union and test) and when it is not. ",
 "a loop over all subsets using list slicing logic and a double loop testing `s & m == s`",[dict(items=[3,1,4,2],target=5),dict(items=[],target=0),dict(items=[1,-1,2],target=0),dict(items=[5],target=3)],BMG)
q(1,'🟡','comb_nodes',"Pruning and the cost of backtracking: count the calls. `candidates` has 1 <= len <= 8 distinct positive integers (each <= 20) and `target` is 1 <= target <= 40. Two recursions find the combinations (unlimited reuse) that sum to `target`. Version A keeps the candidates in the given order: `dfs(start, rem)` counts one call, returns if `rem < 0`, records a hit and returns if `rem == 0`, otherwise loops `j` from `start` to the end and calls `dfs(j, rem - c[j])`. Version B sorts the candidates first and also breaks out of the loop as soon as `c[j] > rem` (so it never calls with a negative remainder). Return `[calls_A, calls_B, hits]` (counting the first call `dfs(0, target)`, and `hits` is the same for both). Explain why B never makes more calls than A, why the two hit counts agree, and give one example where B saves a lot and one where it saves nothing. Do not claim a complexity from these counts; they only measure two implementations on inputs. ",
 "the same two recursions rewritten with an explicit stack",[dict(candidates=[5,2,3],target=8),dict(candidates=[7],target=1),dict(candidates=[4,2,3,1],target=10),dict(candidates=[1],target=5)],BTG)
q(1,'🟡','iter_ws',"Iterative backtracking and the recursion limit. `board` is a list of rows (strings of equal length, lowercase) with 1 <= rows * cols <= 2000, and `word` is a string of length 1 to 2000. Return True if `word` can be spelled along a path of horizontally or vertically adjacent cells that uses no cell twice. The single-word recursion of Q9 nests one frame per letter, so a 1 x 1500 row of 'a' with `word = 'a' * 1500` raises RecursionError under CPython's default limit of 1000 (run it and show the message), while the same recursion on a row of 300 works; in this question your solution must be ITERATIVE: keep an explicit stack of (cell, next-direction-index) frames and a set of cells on the path, push when you extend the path, and pop (removing the cell from the set) when all four directions are tried. The tests are corridor-shaped boards (one row, or a winding one-cell-wide path of 'a' bordered by 'b'), on which the search is fast; on an open board of equal letters the search time is exponential for ANY backtracking solution and is out of scope. Say what an explicit stack costs in Python compared with the call stack, and that raising the limit with sys.setrecursionlimit is an observation about one machine and Python version and not a fix you can promise. ",
 "the recursive version of Q9 on boards small enough for its depth",[dict(board=['aab','bba'],word='aba'),dict(board=['abc'],word='cba'),dict(board=['abc'],word='abca'),dict(board=['aaaa'],word='aaaa'),dict(board=['ab','ca'],word='acb')],BTG)
q(1,'🟡','trie_memory',"Trie memory versus a dict of sets. `words` has 1 to 5000 lowercase words (length 1 to 20, repeats allowed). Return `[nodes, slots26, pairs, distinct]`: `nodes` is the number of trie nodes excluding the root; `slots26` is `26 * (nodes + 1)`, the number of child slots if every node, root included, holds a fixed array of 26 children; `pairs` is the number of (prefix, word) pairs you would store in a dict that maps every non-empty prefix to the set of distinct words having that prefix (the sum of the lengths of the distinct words); and `distinct` is the number of distinct words. Explain what each number means in memory terms (dict children store only the existing edges, an array of 26 stores empty slots too, the dict of sets repeats every word under each of its prefixes and its keys are exactly the trie's nodes), why `nodes <= pairs` and when they are equal (exactly when no two distinct words share a first letter), and what a trie does NOT save compared with a plain `set` of words. You may measure `sys.getsizeof` on your machine, labelled as a sandbox measurement for one Python version, but do not present sizes as proof. Part 7 Q41 builds the trie at concept level and Part 3 Q36 compares it with a sorted list for prefix counts; the full API is Q3. ",
 "counting the distinct prefixes of the distinct words with a set",[dict(words=['car','cart','cat','dog','do','car']),dict(words=['a','b','c']),dict(words=['aaa']),dict(words=['ab','ac'])],TRG+" | "+PY)
q(1,'🟡','skip_list',"A minimal skip list (concept level). Implement an ordered set of integers as a skip list with up to 16 levels in which a new node gets level 1, and each further level with probability 1/2 (use a seeded `random.Random`). `ops` is a list (1 <= len <= 2000) of `['insert', x]` (True if x was absent and is now inserted, False if already present), `['contains', x]` and `['remove', x]` (True if x was present and is removed, False otherwise), with |x| <= 10^6. Return the list of results. Describe how a search starts at the top level and drops down, why the expected cost is O(log n) per operation with random levels and O(n) in the worst case, and why the results never depend on the random choices (only the speed does). Say one real use (an ordered in-memory index, for instance a sorted-set structure) and why a balanced tree is the alternative; do not build the whole data store. This is a concept-level question; the Bloom filter is Part 1 Q42 and the LRU idea is Part 6 Q30. ",
 "a Python set tracked next to the skip list",[dict(ops=[['insert',3],['insert',1],['contains',3],['remove',3],['contains',3],['insert',1]]),dict(ops=[['remove',5]]),dict(ops=[['insert',-2],['insert',-2],['remove',-2],['remove',-2]])],ED)
q(1,'🔴','expr_ops',"Expression add operators. `num` is a string of 1 to 8 digits and `target` is an integer with |target| <= 10^9. Insert the binary operators '+', '-' and '*' (or nothing, which joins digits into one multi-digit operand) between the digits so that the expression, evaluated with the usual precedence (multiplication first), equals `target`. An operand may not have a leading zero ('0' alone is fine; '05' and '00' are not). Return every such expression as a string, sorted ascending as Python strings, without duplicates; return an empty list if none exists. Recurse over the cut position while carrying the value so far and the last term, and for '*' use `value - last + last * operand` to undo the last addition; explain why that works and why the carried value avoids re-parsing the string at every leaf. State the upper bound of 4^(n-1) candidate fillings (each of the n - 1 gaps gets '+', '-', '*' or nothing), of which the leading-zero rule removes some. Depth is at most 8. ",
 "enumerating all 4^(n-1) fillings and evaluating each with `eval`",[dict(num='2345',target=25),dict(num='105',target=5),dict(num='00',target=0),dict(num='9',target=9),dict(num='9',target=0)],ED)
